  "id": "kettleworks-field",
  "_note": "The dense map. Twenty by twenty like West Bambulon and nothing like it: forty of its four hundred tiles carry a place or something to read, which is the Look Outside register written down as a budget a test can check. Most of the readables sit off the roads on purpose — a card that opens every time you cross the field is a card a player learns to shut without reading. The Drambus Stack is four tiles of `curd` in the middle, which is its own terrain because a two-hundred-and-ten-foot cheese drawn as rock is a hill. Its door is written as an event and not a gate: the floors behind it are M11.3's, and a gate onto a map this build has not got is a hole where a promise should be. The two pools are ordered on purpose: `draw_enemy` weights a pool so its hardest member is its rarest, so the creature an errand asks for is the *easiest* thing in its region and never the hardest. Getting that backwards is `PLAN.md` §6b row 1 written into content — a set behind the rarest creature, one map further out.",
